W: 1. A blacksmith courted me, nine months or better.
W: He fairly won my heart, wrote me a letter.
W: With his hammer in his hand, he looked so clever.
W: And if I were with my love, I would live forever.
W:
W: 2. Oh, where has my love gone with his cheeks like roses.
W: He's gone across the sea gathering primroses.
W: Will burn and scorch I fear the shining sun his beauty.
W: And if I were with my love, I would do my duty.
W:
W: 3. Strange news is come to town, strange news is carried.
W: Sad news cried up and down, that my love is married.
W: I wish them both much joy, though they can't hear me.
W: And if I were with my love, I'd do my duty.
W:
W: 4. What did you promise me when you lay beside me.
W: You said you'd marry me and not deny me.
W: If I said I'd marry you, 'twas only to try you.
W: So bring your witness, love, and I'll not deny you.
W:
W: 5. Oh, witness I have none, save God Almighty.
W: And may he reward you well for the slighting of me.
W: Her lips grew pale and wan; her heart did tremble.
W: For to think she'd had one love, and he proved deceitful.
